[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: Each ruled term is a narrative in the spec that cites its ruling, and every narrative that uses the term cites it

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[DECISION]
UID: DEC-TERMS-ARE-NARRATIVES
TITLE: Each ruled term is a narrative in the spec that cites its ruling, and every narrative that uses the term cites it
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATUS: accepted
RETIRES_ON: A term needs fields a narrative cannot carry, or the per-term files prove too many to keep.
STATEMENT: >>>
Every ruled term of the glossary -- joint, checkpoint, scribe, sdoc,
strictdoc, canon, beads -- is one NARRATIVE in docs/spec/, TAGS term, whose
TITLE is the word and whose STATEMENT is its ruled meaning. It Cites the
DECISION that ruled it, with a PARENT_FP entry, so a changed ruling dirties
the term. The three glossary rulings move to docs/spec/ with it, because the
vocabulary outlives any plan and a term in the spec may not depend into a
plan.

Any narrative that uses a term Cites the term narrative, with a PARENT_FP
entry, so choosing a different word -- retitling the term -- dirties every
narrative that used it, at the granularity of the narrative that carries the
edge. The glossary a view shows is a narrative whose WIDGET is glossary and
which Contains the terms.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
The operator asked for the glossary to take advantage of dirty edges when
they choose a different word. A term that is only a row in a glossary
cannot be cited, so nothing could depend on it; a term that is a node can.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Ruled 2026-08-30 as a first-pass decision under the operator's delegation of that day ("I'll trust you to take first pass at making decisions"); overrule by superseding. AUTHORED_BY reads llm because the writer has no flag for the human rungs. The skill's glossary table and the README stay as projections of these nodes (INV-SETTLED-STAYS-VISIBLE); generating the table from the nodes is MECH-SDOC-VOCABULARY-SINGLE-SOURCE's, still open.
<<<
